1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,580
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:05,220
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:10,740
My name is Catherine and today we've got an elementary level lesson for you all about getting up in the morning.

4
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:11,300
That's right.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,960
Today we're making breakfast and well, of course, many people have different things when they're making breakfast.

6
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:20,200
Some of them are very big, others are small.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:22,240
So but this is what we're going to talk about today.

8
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:26,120
So why don't we listen to the dialogue and see what they're having for breakfast today.

9
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:32,650
Smells good.

10
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:34,110
What's for breakfast?

11
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:38,470
Well, since we're getting up so late, I decided to make a big breakfast.

12
00:00:38,890 --> 00:00:39,390
Nice.

13
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:41,200
Brunch.

14
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:41,370
Kind of.

15
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:47,250
So I made scrambled and soft boiled eggs, some French toast and buttermilk pancakes.

16
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:49,670
Wow, you really went all out.

17
00:00:50,030 --> 00:00:50,750
Did you make any coffee?

18
00:00:51,130 --> 00:00:52,770
Yeah, just the way you like it.

19
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:56,910
I also put out some cereal and muesli if you feel like having something more light.

20
00:00:57,410 --> 00:00:58,170
Looks good.

21
00:00:58,590 --> 00:01:00,150
I'll squeeze us some fresh orange juice.

22
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,620
Get the jam and butter while you are in there.

23
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:04,300
Oh, and don't forget the syrup.

24
00:01:06,200 --> 00:01:08,480
All righty.

25
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:11,680
All right, we're back.

26
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:12,570
So a lot of interesting things.

27
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:13,590
A huge breakfast.

28
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:15,710
Way too big for me.

29
00:01:16,330 --> 00:01:21,690
I don't like to eat a big breakfast because I feel like it makes me tired and then I don't have any energy.

30
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:23,490
Especially if you just get up, right?

31
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:23,950
Exactly.

32
00:01:24,730 --> 00:01:26,970
You need to shower and maybe have some fruit and then go to work.

33
00:01:27,150 --> 00:01:28,470
But we can talk about that at the end.

34
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:33,450
There were a lot of interesting words and phrases that we heard that we would benefit from taking a closer look at.

35
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:35,990
Language takeaway.

36
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:43,280
All right, so on Language Takeaway today, we're going to take a look at our first word and it's an interesting word.

37
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:44,530
It's kind of a mix of two things.

38
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:45,050
Brunch.

39
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:46,330
That's right.

40
00:01:46,630 --> 00:01:48,410
So this word comes from two different words.

41
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:52,560
Breakfast, b-b-breakfast and lunch.

42
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:52,690
That's right.

43
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:54,230
So brunch.

44
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:58,670
So if you have brunch, what is exactly brunch?

45
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,870
Well there's two ways to look at brunch.

46
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:03,460
One is the time element.

47
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:07,880
Usually brunch is later than breakfast, but maybe a little earlier than lunch.

48
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:09,980
So 11 o'clock or maybe even noon.

49
00:02:11,140 --> 00:02:12,280
But the other thing is size.

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,580
Because you're putting two meals together, usually brunch is very big.

51
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:17,680
You eat a lot of food.

52
00:02:18,140 --> 00:02:21,420
Okay, so it's a perfect combination of breakfast and lunch.

53
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:23,950
I'm the type of person that likes having scrambled eggs at noon.

54
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:26,560
So this is a perfect solution, brunch.

55
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:29,890
Right, you don't have to only eat eggs at breakfast, you can eat them at lunch as well.

56
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:31,120
Exactly.

57
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:34,560
This is actually something we do a lot in America for Mother's Day.

58
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:36,350
So mom wakes up late, we take her to brunch.

59
00:02:36,760 --> 00:02:37,890
It's a very big special meal.

60
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:39,520
Very good.

61
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:47,960
And talking about eggs and the types of eggs that we can have, I mentioned scrambled eggs and in the dialogue we also heard soft-boiled eggs.

62
00:02:48,230 --> 00:02:50,750
Okay, these are two very important kinds of eggs.

63
00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:55,710
Usually if you're in a restaurant, they will ask you how do you want your eggs cooked or done.

64
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:58,390
And my answer is always scrambled.

65
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:05,250
This means someone mixes the eggs before they are cooked and then when they cook, they are hard, they're yellow.

66
00:03:06,030 --> 00:03:08,810
There's no white, it's just yellow and it's a little hard mixture.

67
00:03:10,240 --> 00:03:12,010
But soft-boiled eggs are very, very different.

68
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:18,600
That's right, soft-boiled eggs, you actually don't break the egg or open the egg to cook it like in scrambled eggs.

69
00:03:19,120 --> 00:03:21,860
You actually put the entire egg in boiling water.

70
00:03:22,460 --> 00:03:34,590
I think it's for two minutes and then you take it out and then you open the shell and the eggs are kind of cooked, it's kind of hard in some parts but it's also kind of liquidy in other parts.

71
00:03:34,790 --> 00:03:35,110
That's right.

72
00:03:35,330 --> 00:03:41,350
So the outside, the white part is hard but the yellow part, the yolk is liquid.

73
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:42,690
It's not hard.

74
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:43,350
That's right.

75
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:50,480
And actually if you want the whole egg to be hard, you can ask for hard-boiled eggs.

76
00:03:50,480 --> 00:03:51,790
That's right, those are cooked for much longer.

77
00:03:52,240 --> 00:03:52,390
That's right.

78
00:03:53,240 --> 00:03:58,630
Okay, so we have scrambled eggs, soft-boiled eggs, we have French toast and we have buttermilk pancakes.

79
00:03:59,390 --> 00:04:00,850
Okay, so you can ask for just pancakes.

80
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:09,080
But if you want to be specific, you want to give instructions about what kind of pancakes, you can ask for buttermilk pancakes.

81
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:10,410
And what exactly is buttermilk?

82
00:04:10,850 --> 00:04:19,880
Buttermilk is a very, very creamy, eggy milk that's very, very high in fat that makes everything taste better, I think.

83
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:23,880
This is used a lot in baking, not really in other kinds of foods.

84
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:28,840
So you can have buttermilk muffins or buttermilk biscuits.

85
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:34,300
But usually you have buttermilk pancakes and so these are very rich, very fatty pancakes.

86
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:36,060
Awesome, yeah, they taste delicious.

87
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:44,100
Alright, so now if we want to eat a little bit more healthy, we do have some cereal and some muesli.

88
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:48,330
Muesli is a funny word, it's not a sound we make very often in English.

89
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:49,210
Muesli.

90
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:50,840
Yeah.

91
00:04:50,840 --> 00:04:51,610
I think it actually comes from German.

92
00:04:52,570 --> 00:04:53,470
It does look German.

93
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,150
Because actually the spelling is M-U-E, so you would kind of want to say Muesli or something.

94
00:05:00,510 --> 00:05:11,180
Yeah, no, we say Muesli and it's basically a mixture of granola or different kinds of fiber grains.

95
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:22,040
So just think of having raisins and maybe some almonds, granola, all mixed up and you have this nice thing that goes well with yogurt, muesli.

96
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:24,040
It's very, very tasty and also very good for you.

97
00:05:25,200 --> 00:05:33,040
And now, well, actually going back to pancakes, no pancake is possible to eat unless you put syrup on it, right?

98
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:34,220
Well, some people might disagree.

99
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:37,460
I know a lot of people who eat pancakes with honey or with butter.

100
00:05:38,720 --> 00:05:40,320
But the best, I think, is with syrup.

101
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:46,830
And syrup is basically liquid sugar and you put it on your pancakes to make them sweeter.

102
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:54,080
Actually, it's the same thing as honey basically, except that it doesn't come from bees.

103
00:05:54,290 --> 00:06:01,510
Right, so real syrup, we say maple syrup, comes from maple trees and you tap the tree and it comes out.

104
00:06:01,970 --> 00:06:03,050
It's very, very, very sweet.

105
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,640
But we also have syrup you can buy at the store, which is man-made.

106
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:08,640
Artificial.

107
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:10,440
Yeah, it's basically just sugar.

108
00:06:10,440 --> 00:06:11,720
Okay, very good.

109
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:13,600
So that's what we have with pancakes usually.

110
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:16,460
But why don't we go back, listen to our dialogue again.

111
00:06:16,660 --> 00:06:21,220
Let's review all of these yummy breakfast treats and we'll come back in a bit with Fluency Builder.

