126
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:03,800
Alrighty.

127
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:11,600
All right, so now on Fluency Builder, why don't we take a look at a couple of phrases.

128
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:11,860
Fluency Builder.

129
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:17,530
When the guy was kind of surprised, he said, wow, you really went all out.

130
00:07:17,880 --> 00:07:20,830
Okay, so this comes from the phrase, to go all out.

131
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:24,750
So he went all out or I'm going to go all out.

132
00:07:25,800 --> 00:07:30,960
And this means to give something your best, to do more than is expected.

133
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:31,130
That's right.

134
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:41,080
So if you go all out when you're making breakfast, it means you just, you cooked a lot or really good and you spend a lot of time doing it.

135
00:07:41,080 --> 00:07:51,610
Or for example, if it's someone's birthday and I know it's their birthday, I go all out by buying gifts and a cake and lots of decorations and calling all of their friends and having a party.

136
00:07:52,240 --> 00:07:54,110
So this is doing everything for them.

137
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:54,810
That's right.

138
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:56,600
Okay.

139
00:07:56,600 --> 00:08:02,130
And he was really surprised and he really seemed to enjoy breakfast and he said, looks good.

140
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:07,150
Okay, this is a way to say good job or nicely done.

141
00:08:08,080 --> 00:08:10,790
It's almost a way to say, I'm happy about this.

142
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:16,560
Or yeah, and obviously you can also say that the food looks delicious, so it looks good.

143
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:17,560
It looks good.

144
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:22,770
So it's a way to say, you know, well done, looks good or in this case, looks very tasty.

145
00:08:23,400 --> 00:08:23,590
That's right.

146
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:25,400
Okay.

147
00:08:25,400 --> 00:08:26,090
And now this is an interesting part.

148
00:08:26,440 --> 00:08:30,070
He says, I'll squeeze us some fresh orange juice.

149
00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:32,250
So let's take a look at this verb, squeeze.

150
00:08:33,320 --> 00:08:33,750
To squeeze.

151
00:08:34,660 --> 00:08:38,130
This is something you usually do with your hand when you tighten something.

152
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:40,950
So for example, your toothpaste.

153
00:08:41,480 --> 00:08:44,290
Yes, you squeeze your toothpaste so the toothpaste will come out.

154
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:49,430
So in this case, we are squeezing the orange juice to extract the juice.

155
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:51,120
That's right.

156
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:58,080
So you, you hold the orange very tight, you make the juice come out and then you have fresh squeezed orange juice.

157
00:08:58,370 --> 00:09:00,350
And this is different from orange juice you buy in a store.

158
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:01,840
Okay.

159
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:05,400
So you can say, I'll squeeze us some fresh orange juice.

160
00:09:05,400 --> 00:09:08,640
That means you're going to make fresh orange juice.

161
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:13,640
And this fresh orange juice that you make is called fresh squeezed orange juice.

162
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:13,940
Fresh squeezed.

163
00:09:14,760 --> 00:09:20,860
You've probably seen this in the store, like some bottles, they say a hundred percent fresh squeezed orange juice or something.

164
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:21,840
Yeah, it's good for advertising.

165
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:23,600
All right.

166
00:09:23,880 --> 00:09:24,760
So very good.

167
